WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.000
Mouth bleed. Bleeding from the mouth can look a bit overwhelming because blood mixes with saliva, but it is usually straightforward to manage.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.000
This type of bleeding can come from the tongue, cheek, lip, or a tooth.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:21.000
If you work with children, keep in mind that losing baby teeth is a completely normal part of growing up, usually starting around age 5 to 7.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:27.000
If a tooth comes out naturally, you can simply tuck it away in a safe spot to give to the child's parent or guardian later.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:32.000
A good way to tell the difference between a natural loss and an injury is to ask,

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:37.000
Did the tooth come out by itself, or did you take a big hit to the mouth?

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:47.000
When an injury occurs, your first step is to apply firm pressure with a clean piece of gauze directly to the source of the bleeding, whether it's the tongue, cheek, or lip.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:52.000
Once the bleeding slows down, check the person's mouth for any chipped or missing teeth.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:55.000
If you find a chipped tooth, you can gently clean it with water.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:00.000
If a tooth has been knocked out, have the person bite down on gauze and call a dentist right away.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.000
To preserve the tooth, place it in a cup or bag filled with a salt solution, milk, or the person's own saliva.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:13.000
As you can see in the diagram on the slide, it is vital that you do not handle the tooth by the roots.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:17.000
You should only touch the crown, which is the top part of the tooth used for chewing.

